        <h1>Exercise Name: Single-Arm Dumbbell Preacher Curl (One-Arm Preacher Curl)</h1>
        <u><h4><b>Exercise Overview</b></h4></u>
        
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates a Single-Arm Dumbbell Preacher Curl. The video highlights the biceps muscle (Biceps Brachii) in bright glowing color to show intense isolation and activation as the arm contracts against the preacher bench pad.

 Exercise Name: Single-Arm Dumbbell Preacher Curl (One-Arm Preacher Curl)
 <b>Target Muscles:</b> Biceps (Biceps Brachii — short and long heads)
 <b>Secondary Muscles:</b> Brachialis, Brachioradialis (forearms)
</div>
</div>

<pre>
<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1>
 <u><h4><b></b>1.Setup: </h4></u></b>Sit at a preacher bench with your chest against the pad. Place the back of your arm firmly against the slanted arm pad, extending your arm fully down while holding a dumbbell with an underhand grip (palm facing up).
 <u><h4><b></b>2.Curling Phase:</h4></u></b> Exhale and contract your biceps to curl the dumbbell upward toward your shoulder. Keep your upper arm glued to the pad throughout the movement to prevent swinging.
 <u><h4><b></b>3.Peak Squeeze:</h4></u></b> Pause briefly at the top position to squeeze your bicep tightly.
 <u><h4><b></b>4.Lowering Phase: </h4></u></b>Inhale as you slowly lower the dumbbell back down over 2 to 3 seconds until your arm is fully extended.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>Strict Isolation:</b> Fixes your upper arm against the pad, eliminating momentum, chest involvement, or shoulder cheating.
 <b>Unilateral Balance:</b> Working one arm at a time allows you to identify and fix strength and size imbalances between arms.
 <b>Enhanced Stretch & Tension:</b> Provides strong mechanical tension, particularly at the bottom stretch position of the bicep.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 per arm | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Definition | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 per arm | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 3 – 4 | 8 – 10 per arm | 90 seconds |


Keep your arm pressed flat on the pad throughout each rep and avoid swinging your torso backward to lift the weight.
